# gnome-desktop -- the shared library several GNOME components build against.
#
# Packaged now because MUTTER NEEDS IT: mutter's -Dlibgnome_desktop defaults
# true and resolves dependency('gnome-desktop-4'), so this has to exist before
# the compositor is attempted. gnome-control-center and gnome-shell reach for it
# too.
#
# Not an LFS package, so it is pinned here rather than in versions.env. BLFS
# 12.4 carries 44.3, and the 44 is not a mistake: gnome-desktop versions
# independently of the GNOME release, so a 44.x beside a 48 series desktop is
# correct. Do not "fix" it to 48.
SRC_URL=https://download.gnome.org/sources/gnome-desktop/44/gnome-desktop-44.3.tar.xz
SRC_SHA256=40efa9aa8d50effed9227a3d70671e32e9dc35e20f331cab3b562975978f4f8d
SRC_DIR=gnome-desktop-44.3

# -Dbuild_gtk4=true IS THE ENTIRE REASON THIS PACKAGE IS BUILT WHEN IT IS.
#
# It defaults true, so this is a pin rather than a change -- but it is the pin
# that matters most here. It produces libgnome-desktop-4.so and, more to the
# point, gnome-desktop-4.pc (libgnome-desktop/meson.build 114 and 152), which is
# the exact name mutter's dependency('gnome-desktop-4') looks for. Turn it off
# and this package still builds, still installs a perfectly good
# libgnome-desktop-3, and mutter fails several waves later against a package
# that looks correct. install.sh asserts the .pc for that reason.
#
# -Dlegacy_library=true, ITS UPSTREAM DEFAULT, AND THIS REVERSES A DECISION THIS
# RECIPE PREVIOUSLY MADE AND JUSTIFIED AT LENGTH.
#
# The previous version turned it OFF, on the reasoning that nothing in this tree
# consumes libgnome-desktop-3.0 -- citing gnome-control-center 48.4
# (meson.build:190), gnome-shell 48.4 (meson.build:88) and mutter 48.4, all of
# which ask for gnome-desktop-4. Every one of those citations is accurate.
#
# THE CHECK WAS SCOPED AND WAS PRESENTED AS COMPLETE. There are five consumers
# of gnome-desktop in this chain, not three; the three checked were the three
# whose tarballs happened to be on hand at the time. The other two require the
# GTK 3 library, unconditionally:
#
#     gnome-session 48.0        meson.build:97   gnome-desktop-3.0 >= 3.34.2
#     gnome-settings-daemon 48.1 meson.build:104 gnome-desktop-3.0 >= 3.37.1
#
# Neither carries required:false. So the desktop session does not start without
# the library this recipe had disabled -- and it would have been discovered at
# gnome-session's first CI run, two packages and a publish cycle later, as a
# missing .pc naming gnome-desktop rather than naming the decision.
#
# The lesson is worth more than the flag: a consumer check is only as complete
# as the consumer set, and "I checked every consumer" means "I checked every
# consumer I had". Anyone re-examining this option should enumerate consumers
# from the ownership list, not from what is unpacked in /tmp.
#
# -Dudev=enabled and -Dsystemd=enabled ARE PINNED EVEN THOUGH BOTH ALREADY
# RESOLVE CORRECTLY ON THEIR OWN, and the reason is worth stating because it
# looks redundant. Both are meson `feature` options declared with NO value, so
# both default to **auto**:
#
#   meson.build:67  libsystemd_dep = dependency('libsystemd', required: get_option('systemd'))
#   meson.build:68  udev_dep       = dependency('libudev',    required: get_option('udev'))
#
# eudev provides libudev, and elogind installs a libsystemd.pc symlink
# precisely so GNOME components find that name -- so auto would enable both, and
# that IS what is wanted. But an auto feature does not record a decision; it
# records whatever the build image happened to contain. Two auto features left
# alone are two silent dependencies on image contents, and the first time one of
# them is absent the result is a quietly reduced library rather than a failure.
#
# -Ddesktop_docs=true is kept, its default, and it is NOT the trap it resembles.
# desktop-docs/{fdl,gpl,lgpl}/meson.build each call gnome.yelp(), which is
# meson's own GNOME module and needs itstool and msgfmt -- both packaged. No
# yelp-tools, no new dependency.
#
# BUT NOTE WHY itstool IS DECLARED EXPLICITLY in [dependencies.build]: a
# gnome.yelp() call is a meson MODULE call, not a find_program(), so
# tools/check-recipes.sh's scan of the tarball cannot see it. The tool reads
# calls, not modules. Without the explicit declaration nothing anywhere would
# record that this build needs itstool.
#
# -Ddebug_tools=false: defaults TRUE and builds test and debug binaries that are
# of no use in a shipped desktop.
#
# NOT SET, deliberately: -Dgtk_doc and -Dinstalled_tests are already false
# upstream. Recorded so a future default flip reads as a change rather than as a
# silent new dependency.
#
# ONE APPARENT SHORTFALL THAT IS NOT ONE, recorded so nobody bumps it: BLFS
# names xkeyboard-config 2.45 and this tree ships 2.44.0. meson.build:64 is
# `dependency('xkeyboard-config')` with NO VERSION ARGUMENT AT ALL, so there is
# no floor to miss -- 2.45 is simply what BLFS happened to build against.
MESON_ARGS="-Dbuild_gtk4=true -Dlegacy_library=true \
-Dudev=enabled -Dsystemd=enabled \
-Ddesktop_docs=true -Ddebug_tools=false -Dintrospection=true"
